{"subjectId":"c","id":"string-functions","title":"string.h Functions","group":"Arrays & Strings","order":21,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>&lt;string.h&gt;</code> provides string (<code>str…</code>) and raw memory (<code>mem…</code>) functions. None of them allocate memory or check destination sizes — you supply buffers big enough.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Does</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>strlen(s)</code></td><td>Length excluding <code>\\0</code></td><td>O(n)</td></tr><tr><td><code>strcpy(d, s)</code> / <code>strncpy(d, s, n)</code></td><td>Copy</td><td><code>strcpy</code> overflows if <code>d</code> is too small; <code>strncpy</code> may <strong>not</strong> NUL-terminate</td></tr><tr><td><code>strcat(d, s)</code> / <code>strncat(d, s, n)</code></td><td>Append</td><td><code>strncat</code> always terminates; <code>n</code> = max chars to append</td></tr><tr><td><code>strcmp(a, b)</code> / <code>strncmp</code></td><td>Compare</td><td>&lt;0, 0, &gt;0 — not just −1/0/1</td></tr><tr><td><code>strchr(s, c)</code> / <code>strrchr</code></td><td>First / last occurrence of a char</td><td>Returns pointer or <code>NULL</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>strstr(h, n)</code></td><td>Find substring</td><td>Pointer or <code>NULL</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>strspn</code> / <code>strcspn</code></td><td>Length of prefix made of / not made of a set</td><td><code>s[strcspn(s, \"\\n\")] = 0</code> strips a newline</td></tr><tr><td><code>strtok(s, delims)</code></td><td>Split into tokens</td><td>Modifies <code>s</code>, keeps hidden static state — not reentrant</td></tr><tr><td><code>memcpy(d, s, n)</code></td><td>Copy <code>n</code> bytes</td><td>Regions must <strong>not</strong> overlap</td></tr><tr><td><code>memmove(d, s, n)</code></td><td>Copy <code>n</code> bytes</td><td>Overlap-safe</td></tr><tr><td><code>memset(p, byte, n)</code></td><td>Fill bytes</td><td>Sets <em>bytes</em>, not ints</td></tr><tr><td><code>memcmp(a, b, n)</code></td><td>Compare bytes</td><td>Don’t use on structs (padding bytes)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-copy\"></i>Copy, concatenate, compare</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;string.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    char buf[32];\n    strcpy(buf, \"Hello\");\n    strcat(buf, \", \");\n    strncat(buf, \"world!!!\", 6);          /* append at most 6 chars */\n    printf(\"%s (%zu)\\n\", buf, strlen(buf));\n\n    printf(\"%d %d %d\\n\", strcmp(\"apple\", \"apple\") == 0,\n           strcmp(\"apple\", \"banana\") &lt; 0, strcmp(\"b\", \"a\") &gt; 0);\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", strncmp(\"prefix-match\", \"prefix-other\", 6) == 0);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>Hello, world! (13)\n1 1 1\n1</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-search\"></i>Searching</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;string.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    const char *path = \"/usr/local/bin/gcc\";\n    const char *slash = strrchr(path, '/');\n    printf(\"file: %s\\n\", slash + 1);\n    printf(\"dir length: %td\\n\", slash - path);\n\n    const char *hit = strstr(path, \"local\");\n    printf(\"found \\\"local\\\" at index %td\\n\", hit - path);\n    printf(\"%s\\n\", strchr(path, 'z') == NULL ? \"no z\" : \"has z\");\n    printf(\"vowel-free prefix of \\\"strength\\\": %zu\\n\", strcspn(\"strength\", \"aeiou\"));\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>file: gcc\ndir length: 14\nfound \"local\" at index 5\nno z\nvowel-free prefix of \"strength\": 3</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-cut\"></i>Tokenising with strtok</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;string.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    char csv[] = \"name,,age;city\";              /* must be writable */\n    for (char *tok = strtok(csv, \",;\"); tok; tok = strtok(NULL, \",;\"))\n        printf(\"[%s] \", tok);                    /* empty fields are skipped */\n    printf(\"\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>[name] [age] [city]</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-cpu\"></i>Memory functions</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;string.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int a[5] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}, b[5];\n    memcpy(b, a, sizeof a);                  /* copy the whole array */\n\n    memmove(a + 1, a, 3 * sizeof a[0]);      /* overlapping shift right */\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 5; i++) printf(\"%d \", a[i]);\n    printf(\"\\n\");\n\n    memset(b, 0, sizeof b);                  /* zero is safe for any type */\n    printf(\"%d %d\\n\", b[0], b[4]);\n\n    int ones[2];\n    memset(ones, 1, sizeof ones);            /* sets BYTES to 1: 0x01010101 */\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", ones[0]);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>1 1 2 3 5\n0 0\n16843009</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i>Buffer overflow with strcpy</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C — stack-buffer-overflow caught by AddressSanitizer</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;string.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    char small[8];\n    const char *input = \"this is far too long\";\n    strcpy(small, input);          /* writes 21 bytes into 8 */\n    printf(\"%s\\n\", small);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C — the safe version</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;string.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    char small[8];\n    const char *input = \"this is far too long\";\n    snprintf(small, sizeof small, \"%s\", input);   /* truncates, always terminates */\n    printf(\"%s\\n\", small);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>this is</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>strncpy(d, s, n)</code> does <strong>not</strong> add <code>'\\0'</code> when <code>s</code> has <code>n</code> or more characters, and it pads with zeros otherwise. Prefer <code>snprintf(d, size, \"%s\", s)</code> or set <code>d[n - 1] = '\\0'</code> yourself.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the difference between memcpy and memmove?</summary><div><p>Both copy <code>n</code> bytes. <code>memcpy</code> assumes the source and destination do not overlap (undefined behaviour if they do), so it can copy in any order and is slightly faster. <code>memmove</code> handles overlap correctly, as if copying through a temporary buffer.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why is strtok considered unsafe?</summary><div><p>It modifies the input string (writes <code>\\0</code> over delimiters), cannot be used on string literals, and keeps a hidden static pointer between calls — so it is not thread-safe and you cannot tokenise two strings at once. POSIX <code>strtok_r</code> takes the state as a parameter.</p></div></details></section>"}